response:
Sure, a "loop" is a way of repeating a set of commands for a certain number of times or until a certain condition is met.  For example, you might use a loop to print out the first 50 prime numbers.  You can think of it like a stream of commands that will always perform the same action, but the number of times it performs that action will be variable.  In C, you can use a "for" loop or a "while" loop, but for most cases you will want to use the "for" loop.  The "for" loop is a set of commands that have some fixed amount of iterations, and you specify the conditions under which the loop should be exited.  Here’s an example:

int i = 0;
int n = 1000;
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i == 50) {
        break;  // exit the loop when i is equal to 50
    }
    printf("%d ", n);
    n--;
}

This loop will execute ten times (one for each number from 0 to 50 inclusive).  The "if" statement tests if i is equal to 50, and if that’s true the loop is exited.  Otherwise the loop will execute, printing out each number from 0 to n-1, and then decrementing n, giving us the next number in the sequence in the next iteration.

